cartridge "Q-BIRD/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1986"

' Q-BIRD
' A ColecoVision game based on "Q-Bird", COMPUTE!'s Gazette December 1986
' (Commodore 64 machine language, one file started with SYS 2061; the
' disk and the program do not name the author). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a baby bluebird, too young to fly, hops from cube to cube on a
' slanted board of 7 x 6 cube tops. Each landing changes a top's colour one
' step toward the colour shown at the bottom (CHANGE TO); turn every top to
' it to clear the level. Hop off the edge and you fall. A cat stalks you,
' toads hop about at random, a hawk sweeps across one row, a marble rolls
' down the slope. Hold the button while you push the stick for a flapping
' long jump: two cubes at once, and nothing can touch you in mid-air.
'
' From the original (orig/qbird.dis, read from the code and timed in
' jsc64): the board of 7 x 6 tops in rows shifted one step per row; the
' four hop directions (stick up = up the slope, down = down the slope,
' left, right) of 25 one-pixel steps a jiffy; fire held doubles every step
' (the long jump) and makes you safe from the enemies in mid-hop; a top
' changes colour one step per landing and stops at the level's colour,
' level N needing N steps (level select on the title with the stick, 1 to
' 15); 10 points per colour step times the step number; a level ends when
' all 42 tops are done; three random hoppers that only land on tops, a
' chaser that hops toward your row first and then along it, a bird that
' flies across a random row from the left, a ball that drops down the
' slope, a bonus item on a random top (100 points, the enemies stop for a
' while); hopping off the board loses a life; five lives shown, the game
' ends on a death with none shown; GET READY, GAME OVER, a high score.
'
' Liberties: original drawings (a bluebird, a cat, toads, a hawk, a glass
' marble, a worm as the bonus) on cubes with a front face; the tops are
' squared (the C64 drew slanted ones); hops follow an arc (the C64 drew a
' three-segment path); the long jump is chosen when a hop starts (the C64
' read the button every step); levels need at most 4 colour steps a top
' (the C64 went up to 15, which needs 630 landings): from level 5 the
' enemies are faster instead; the number of toads grows from 2 to 3 after
' level 1; the enemies go back to their starting places after a death so
' the bird is not caught at once again (the C64 left them where they
' were); the hawk and the marble wait a few seconds before coming back;
' the chaser stays on the board; collisions are box tests on the sprites'
' places (the C64 compared positions too); sounds for every hop, landing,
' fall and catch; the title tune; GAME OVER then the title (the C64
' restarted after a delay).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick hops; hold a side button with
' the stick for the long jump. On the title, up and down choose the level,
' a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. Own xorshift random numbers.

